aying: if angle at A is right, then BC² = AB² + AC², which is written in Lean as:\n|(b─c)|*|(b─c)| = |(a─b)|*|(a─b)| + |(a─c)|*|(a─c)|\n\nNow, let's formalize:\n\n```tactics\ntheorem example_thm (a b c : Point) (AB BC AC : Line) (h1 : formTriangle a b c AB BC AC) (h2 : (∠ b:a:c : ℝ) = ∟) : |(b─c)|*|(b─c)| = |(a─b)|*|(a─b)| + |(a─c)|*|(a─c)| := by sorry\n```\n\nWait, hold on again. Let's think more carefully. The hypotheses are:\n\n- a, b, c are points\n- AB, BC, AC are lines\n- h1 : formTriangle a b c AB BC AC\n- h2 : angle b:a:c is right angle (∟)\n\nNow the conclusion is that the square on side opposite to angle A (which is angle at a between sides AB and AC, so side BC) is equal to sum of squares on sides forming the right angle (which are AB and AC).\n\nSo, side opposite to angle A is |(b─c)|, side from a to b is |(a─b)|, and side from a to c is |(a─c)|. So it's correct that the theorem is saying:\n\n|(b─c)| * |(b─c)| = |(a─b)| * |(a─b)| + |(a─c)| * |(a─c)|\n\nThis is exactly the Pythagorean theorem. \n\nNow, in Lean, to express the relationship about the squares. But here we are told angle b:a:c is a right angle. So that angle is right, thus the side |(b─c)| is the hypotenuse, and |(a─b)| and |(a─c)| are the legs, so indeed:\n\n|(b─c)|² = |(a─b)|² + |(a─c)|²\n\nSo the theorem is correctly formalized then, with angle at A (∠ b:a:c) being ∟, hence side |(b─c)| is the side that is equal in square to the sum of squares on |(a─b)| and |(a─c)|.\n\nNow, in Lean 4:\n\n```tactics\ntheorem example_thm (a b c : Point) (AB BC AC : Line) (h1 : formTriangle a b c AB BC AC) (h2 : (∠ b:a:c : ℝ) = ∟) : |(b─c)|*|(b─c)| = |(a─b)|*|(a─b)| + |(a─c)|*|(a─c)| := by sorry\n```\n\nBut we need to continue from here. The theorem is now formalized with the right angle given at angle b:a:c, so side b-c is the one that its square is equal to sum of squares on a-b and a-c.\n</think>\n```lean4\ntheorem example_thm (a b c : Point) (AB BC AC : Line) (h1 : formTriangle a b c AB BC AC) (h2 : (∠ b